er":null},{"season":2023,"team":"IND","distance":0.6836,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"CHI","distance":0.7178,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"},{"season":2021,"team":"PHI","distance":0.7223,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jonathan Gannon"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The 49ers went 12-5 in 2025 and outscored opponents 437 to 371. The offense carried the team, ranking 6th in EPA per play while the defense finished 24th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the highest 21 personnel usage and the 2nd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The 49ers offense was well above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the highest 21 personnel usage and the 2nd highest pre-snap motion rate in the league. Mac Jones ran the passing game, with Christian McCaffrey leading the team in targets.","defense":"The 49ers defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was sack rate, where it ranked 32nd. Nick Bosa was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Bryce Huff, who had 4 sacks while playing only 56% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the 2nd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The 49ers were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 92% of field goals, which was well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 7% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job was split, with Mac Jones and Brock Purdy both getting meaningful snaps. Christian McCaffrey was the clear top target with 24% of the looks and 924 receiving yards. Christian McCaffrey handled most of the work in the backfield with 76% of the carries. On defense, Bryce Huff led the team with 4 sacks and Deommodore Lenoir picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Kyle Shanahan called the offense as head coach and Robert Saleh called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2025 Patriots under Josh McDaniels. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Texans under DeMeco Ryans and the 2020 Colts.","trends":"The offense went from 10th in EPA per play in 2024 to 6th in 2025, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 23rd in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 24th in 2025, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.27 to 6.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}